    {
      "page_title": "Data Loss Risks and Zero-Downtime Deploys",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "MISTAKE: LOSING DATA",
          "style": "ribbon"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Database as a lone container, no volume, no backups",
            "Container removed or host lost means data gone",
            "Fix: named volume, scheduled dumps, or a managed database"
          ],
          "style": "cross",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "red"
        },
        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "1. Zero-Downtime Deploys",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "bullets",
          "items": [
            "Rolling update replaces replicas a few at a time",
            "Healthchecks gate traffic to each new replica",
            "Roll back fast if the new version fails checks"
          ],
          "style": "check",
          "frame": "solid",
          "ink": "blue"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Compose vs Kubernetes",
          "text": "Compose suits one host; Kubernetes adds self-healing and rolling updates."
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "Interview Point",
          "items": [
            "Why does Kubernetes replace Compose in production?",
            "How do you do zero-downtime deploys?",
            "What does a HEALTHCHECK do?"
          ],
          "ink": "purple"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "banner",
          "title": "KEY TAKEAWAYS:",
          "text": "Health-check it, limit it, stop it on SIGTERM, log to stdout, orchestrate it."
        }
      ],
      "hero": "docker",
      "subtitle": "Run it, stop it, scale it"
